in the creation of the monumental Temple of Artemis at Ephesus."}, {"sourceSlug": "classical-greek-architecture", "sourceName": "Classical Greek Architecture", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "paeonius-of-ephesus", "targetName": "Paeonius of Ephesus", "context": "The established principles of Classical Greek architecture profoundly influenced the designs and techniques employed by Paeonius of Ephesus in his work."}, {"sourceSlug": "paeonius-of-ephesus", "sourceName": "Paeonius of Ephesus", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "hellenistic-architecture", "targetName": "Hellenistic Architecture", "context": "The grand scale and intricate details of Paeonius of Ephesus's work on the Artemision influenced the subsequent development of Hellenistic architectural styles."}], "places": [{"name": "Ephesus, Asia Minor", "role": "Primary workplace"}, {"name": "Ionia, Asia Minor", "role": "Geographic region"}, {"name": "Ancient Greece", "role": "Cultural origin"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Paeonius of Ephesus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "paeonius-of-ephesus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q9058133", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paeonius_of_Ephesus"], "tags": ["Greece", "Architects & Engineers", "Europe", "innovation-and-technology", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Paeonius of Ephesus's involvement in the design and construction of the Temple of Artemis contributed to one of the ancient world's most celebrated architectural achievements, influencing subsequent temple designs and solidifying Ephesus's cultural prominence. His work, alongside collaborators, demonstrated the pinnacle of Classical Greek monumental construction.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-14T04:39:01Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31770425448", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Paeonius of Ephesus was an ancient Greek architect, one of the builders of the temple of Artemis at Ephesus.", "newValue": "Paeonius of Ephesus was a distinguished ancient Greek architect, renowned for his significant contributions to the Temple of Artemis at Ephesus, one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World. Flourishing in the late Classical period, Paeonius's work on this colossal Ionic structure, alongside Demetrius, epitomized the ambitious scale and refined aesthetic of Greek monumental architecture. His precise birthdate remains unknown, but his death is recorded around 379 BCE, placing his active career in the early 4th century BCE and potentially earlier.

Paeonius's primary achievement centered on the Artemision, a temple dedicated to the Greek goddess Artemis. While the temple underwent multiple iterations and a famous rebuilding after Herostratus's arson in 356 BCE, Paeonius is traditionally credited with an influential role in its design and construction, likely on an earlier phase or the overall conception that guided subsequent efforts. The temple was celebrated for its immense size, featuring over 120 columns, each reaching approximately 18 meters in height, adorned with intricate sculptures and a dazzling white marble facade. His involvement underscores the collaborative nature of major architectural projects in antiquity, where master builders often oversaw vast teams of craftsmen and laborers.

